+# Provisional cubic Fokker--Planck collision source
+
+## Scope
+
+The prototype in `src/numerics/fp_collision35.jl` projects FPCode's continuous
+cubic Fokker--Planck velocity generator onto Riemann35's existing 35 raw
+moments. It is intentionally CPU-only and is not wired into
+`simulation_runner` or the GPU kernels.
+
+The retained/evolved state is unchanged: all monomials through total degree
+four. This is not a sixth-order moment system. Degree-five and degree-six
+moments appear only as algebraic values needed to evaluate the collision
+source of the retained fourth-order moments.
+
+## Provisional tail model
+
+The lookup used by the source is
+
+| Degree | Source |
+|---|---|
+| 0--4 | exact retained Riemann35 state |
+| 5 | analytical `Moments5_3D` HyQMOM closure |
+| 6 | moments of the non-negative `chyqmom_nodes_3d` quadrature |
+
+The M6 choice is a diagnostic model, not a production claim. The conditional
+quadrature documents a structurally truncated subset of high-order cross
+constraints even at M4. For that reason it is used only to supply M6; it never
+overwrites the retained state or the analytical M5 closure.
+
+## Hyperbolicity and realizability boundary
+
+Because M6 is not transported, this prototype does not alter Riemann35's M5
+flux closure or its flux Jacobian. Transport hyperbolicity remains the
+responsibility of the existing Riemann35 method. The new risk is instead that
+a nonlinear collision-source update can move an M4 state outside the
+realizable cone.
+
+`fp_collision35` is therefore only a validation integrator. It retries an
+explicit step with successively finer substeps until every accepted state
+passes `is_realizable`. A production integration needs a measured source
+integrator and a device-compatible M6 closure; neither is claimed here.
+
+## Collision model and invariants
+
+In nondimensional units (`molecular mass = k_B = 1`), the peculiar-velocity
+drift is
+
+```text
+a(c) = -c/tau + C*c + Gamma*(|c|^2 - 5*theta)
+       + Beta*(|c|^2*c - 2*q/rho),
+```
+
+with isotropic velocity diffusion `theta/tau`. `C`, `Gamma`, and `Beta` use
+the analytical coefficient map in FPCode. The source is projected so that
+mass, all three momentum components, and the trace of the second raw moment
+are conserved to roundoff.
+
+This is the unclipped continuous-time polynomial model. It deliberately omits
+the particle implementation's `m2_lim` speed cutoff and finite-step alpha
+rescaling. Those operations are not polynomial moment closures; their effect
+must be measured in the direct FPCode particle comparison rather than silently
+folded into the first analytical source.
+
+## Acceptance gates before solver wiring
+
+1. Maxwellian stationarity in the Ornstein--Uhlenbeck limit (`Pr=1`).
+2. Exact collision invariants for a non-equilibrium Gaussian mixture.
+3. Realizability of accepted homogeneous source steps.
+4. Direct comparison against the existing FPCode particle reference.
+5. Sensitivity of M4 relaxation to the M6 model.
+6. Guidance/review of the algebraic M6 construction before crossing-jet runs.
+
+Only after these gates should a collision-model selector be added to the CPU
+solver. GPU work follows a CPU-validated, allocation-free tail implementation.
+
+## Focused validation command
+
+From the Riemann35 repository root on a machine with Julia installed:
+
+```bash
+HYQMOM_SKIP_PLOTTING=true CI=true julia --project=. -e \
+  'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate(); using Riemann35; include("test/test_fp_collision35.jl")'
+```
+
+The focused test does not run a spatial solver. It checks the closure sizes,
+finite source values, Maxwellian stationarity, the five collision invariants,
+and realizability after one small homogeneous collision step.
